-<style id="c3-nohscroll">html{overflow-x:clip}#c3-fab{display:none !important}</style><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":"A Myrtle Beach Condo in Litigation Can Be the Best Deal","description":"Most buyers are told a flat no. When you can buy a condo that is in active litigation on the Grand Strand, why lenders decline it, and how one Myrtle Beach buyer got a steep discount on a like-new oceanfront unit.","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","datePublished":"2026-07-10","dateModified":"2026-10-27","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#devin-day"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"mainEntityOfPage":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/condo-in-litigation/"}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#devin-day","name":"Devin Day","jobTitle":"Operations Officer","description":"Operations Officer","worksFor":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"url":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/team/devin.webp"}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://chapter3realty.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Buyers","item":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Condo in Litigation","item":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/condo-in-litigation/"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Can you get a mortgage on a condo in active litigation?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Usually not with conventional, FHA, or VA financing, because active litigation fails the warrantability standard, the condo-project rules those loans require. But the deal is not always dead: if the litigation is close to resolving, you can sometimes structure the contract to close once it clears, or use a non-warrantable / portfolio loan."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why do lenders decline condos in litigation?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac, FHA, and VA worry that a lawsuit could end in a large special assessment, or in the worst case a condemned building, that would damage the value of the collateral. They decline the whole project until the litigation resolves."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a condo in litigation always mean you should walk away?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. The details matter: what the suit is about, whether the repairs are already done, and how close it is to resolving. A building that has already fixed the defects and is near the end of its litigation can be a rare discount on a like-new project that most buyers cannot touch yet."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What does it mean when a condo is non-warrantable?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A condo project that fails the project rules behind normal conventional and government loans, often because of litigation, a high investor-ownership ratio, or commercial space. These need a non-warrantable or portfolio loan, which is exactly the kind of financing our preferred lender can walk you through."}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "RealEstateAgent", "@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/#org", "name": "Chapter3 Realty", "legalName": "Chapter3 Realty Corp", "employee": [{"@type": "Person", "name": "Devin Day", "jobTitle": "Operations Officer", "image": "https://chapter3realty.com/team/devin.webp", "url": "https://chapter3realty.com/about/"}, {"@type": "Person", "name": "Timothy Nash", "alternateName": "Tim Nash", "jobTitle": "Broker-in-Charge", "image": "https://chapter3realty.com/team/timmy.webp", "url": "http