+<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/">Buyers</a><span>/</span><a href="/buyers/relocating/">Relocating</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Why Myrtle Beach</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">The big picture</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Why everyone is moving to Myrtle Beach:<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">four reasons, told with numbers.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 1, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">About 15,000 households a year move to the Myrtle Beach area: a typical home near $342,000, low taxes on a primary home, and a coast that stays warm into October.</p><div style="margin-top:1.8rem;display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="#lead-form">Talk to a local expert</a><a class="btn btn-outline btn-lg" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Four reasons, told with numbers</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">People move here for the same four reasons in different orders. A typical home costs about $342,000 while the states our buyers come from average $418,000 to $669,000 on the same measure. South Carolina taxes are among the friendliest in the country for a primary home: property tax near 0.38 percent in Horry County, Social Security never taxed, no estate tax. The coast works twelve months, with the ocean warm into October and winters mild enough that Devin rides his motorcycle into December. Jobs, stores, hospitals and neighborhoods are being built at the same time, so arriving does not mean settling for less.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The numbers behind that paragraph: the Census Bureau ranked this metro among the fastest growing in the country in March 2026. The IRS counted about 15,000 households arriving in a single year, with about 2.5 times the income of the households leaving. Employment grew 9.1 percent from 2019 to 2025 against 5.1 percent nationally. Each figure is sourced on its own page, and this page links them all.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Four reasons, told with numbers</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">People move here for the same four reasons in different orders. A typical home costs about $342,000 while the states our buyers come from average $418,000 to $669,000 on the same measure. South Carolina taxes are among the friendliest in the country for a primary home: property tax near 0.38 percent in Horry County, Social Security never taxed, no estate tax. The coast works twelve months, with the ocean warm into October and winters mild enough that one of our team rides his motorcycle into December. Jobs, stores, hospitals and neighborhoods are being built at the same time, so arriving does not mean settling for less.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The numbers behind that paragraph: the Census Bureau ranked this metro among the fastest growing in the country in March 2026. The IRS counted about 15,000 households arriving in a single year, with about 2.5 times the income of the households leaving. Employment grew 9.1 percent from 2019 to 2025 against 5.1 percent nationally. Each figure is sourced on its own page, and this page links them all.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">From the Northeast</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Why Northeasterners move to Myrtle Beach</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Connecticut and Massachusetts send us the same story with different numbers: sell a house that costs too much to keep, in a place that taxes too much of everything, and buy the coast outright. A Massachusetts average home sale is close to double a home here. New Jersey property tax bills fall by thousands a year. Connecticut retirees stop watching the income lines that decide whether their Social Security is taxed, because here it never is.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">One of our Massachusetts buyers, a retired Boston hotel manager, turned a $450,000 house sale into three oceanfront condos and a working beach life his friends came down to copy; his story is on the <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-massachusetts/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Massachusetts page</a>. The full math, state by state: <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-new-york/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">New York</a>, <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-new-jersey/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">New Jersey</a>, <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-pennsylvania/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Pennsylvania</a>, <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-connecticut/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Connecticut</a> and <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-massachusetts/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Massachusetts</a>.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">From the Midwest</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Why Midwesterners move to Myrtle Beach</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ohio buyers, and Devin is one, tend to name two things first. The paycheck stops losing its city slice, because South Carolina has no local income tax anywhere, while most Ohio cities take one or two percent of every check. Weather is the other. The nearest long-term weather station here records 111 clear days a year against 66 for Cleveland. Winters here are mild enough that the beach stays a daily option.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Devin&#39;s own move, with the vehicle tax that surprised him and the winters that did not, is written out on the <a href="/buyers/relocating/from-ohio/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Ohio page</a>, and the <a href="/buyers/relocating/weather/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">weather page</a> carries every season in government numbers.</p></div></section>
