 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get the 4 percent tax rate on a second home in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina gives the 4 percent rate only to the home your household lives in, and the owner certifies that no one in the household is a legal resident of another state. A second home is taxed on 6 percent of its value."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I use a rental manager with a second-home loan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, as long as no agreement gives the manager control over who stays in the home and when. The lender also cannot count the rent toward qualifying you for a second-home loan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who looks after a second home while I am away?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A local property manager can check on the home, handle repairs and, if you rent it, run the bookings. Chapter3 sells homes and does not manage them, and our property management page explains what managers here charge."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What about hurricane season?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Atlantic hurricane season runs from June 1 to November 30. A beach house needs a wind policy and usually flood coverage, and a condo owner relies on the building's master policy. We check the flood zone of any home before you make an offer."}}]}</script>
