 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/benefits/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are HOA fees worth it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For most condo buyers here, yes, because you buy as a group. The dues pay for insurance on the buildings, the roof and the grounds at prices one owner cannot get. The cost is control. If reserves are funded and the rules fit your plan, the dues are buying real work."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do HOA fees cover insurance on my condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They cover the buildings and the shared parts, not always the inside of your unit. South Carolina law makes a condo association insure the property. Your lender will require your own unit policy when the group policy leaves your interior out or charges a deductible per unit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are HOA fees tax deductible?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not on a home you live in. On a rental condo, dues that pay to maintain the shared parts are usually deductible as a rental expense. A special assessment for an improvement is not treated the same way. This is general information and not tax advice. Ask a CPA."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an HOA raise fees without telling owners?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An HOA that is not a nonprofit corporation must give at least 48 hours' notice of the meeting where the budget increase is decided. An HOA incorporated as a nonprofit follows its bylaws, and state law requires no notice to owners before its board meets. Nothing caps the size of the increase. Read the bylaws, because they decide how much notice you get."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if I do not pay my HOA fees?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a condo, unpaid dues become a lien on the unit, and that lien can be foreclosed like a mortgage. The debt follows the unit when it 