der-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How do I find out if a unit has unpaid assessments before I buy?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Request an estoppel or resale statement from the association or its management company. It states what is owed on that specific unit as of a date. Never rely on the seller's recollection.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Can I withhold dues if the HOA is not doing its job?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">It is a bad idea. Withholding usually creates a debt and a lien without resolving the underlying dispute. If you believe the association has failed in its obligations, that is a question for a South Carolina attorney.</p></div></div></section>
