 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/non-warrantable-condos/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a non-warrantable condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A warrantable condo is one that qualifies for a normal conventional loan backed by Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac. A non-warrantable condo is one that does not, because the building fails one or more of their project rules. You can still buy and finance a non-warrantable condo, but you use a different type of loan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What makes a condo non-warrantable in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The most common reasons on the Grand Strand are hotel-style rental operations, a master insurance policy that no longer meets the conventional-loan rules, and active structural litigation. Others are low reserves or unpaid dues, one owner holding too many units, too much commercial space, or needed repairs the building has not made. The reason matters, because it decides which loan you can use and whether the building is still a smart buy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you get a loan on a non-warrantable condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Conventional and government loans will not work, but portfolio loans, non-QM loans, DSCR loans, and bank statement loans can. Plan on a larger down payment than a standard condo and a higher rate. Your exact terms depend on why the building is non-warrantable and which loan fits."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I use an FHA or VA loan on a non-warrantable condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. FHA, VA, and USDA loans require the condo project to be on their approved list, and a non-warrantable building is not. Normal conventional loans do not work either. That leaves portfolio and non-QM loans, which is what most non-warrantable condos close with."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why are more Myrtle Beach oceanfront condos non-warrantable now?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A conventional-lending rule updated in early 2024 requires a condo's master insurance policy to cover 100 percent of replacement cost and to carry a deductible no higher than 5 percent of that coverage. Many coastal buildings can only buy wind or named-storm coverage with deductibles above 5 percent, so their master policy fails and Fannie flags the whole building ineligible. Buildings that have not updated their policy are being flagged by this."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out if a condo building is warrantable before I make an offer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Have a lender review the building before you write the offer, not after. Our licensed agents pull the HOA documents and our licensed mortgage team reads them and tells you whether the building is likely to be ruled non-warrantable. The underwriter makes the final call. A building's recent sales are a strong signal. If a buyer closed a conventional loan there last week, you likely can too."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a non-warrantable condo a bad investment?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not always. A non-warrantable condo is often cheaper because financing is harder, which means fewer buyers and less competition. If the reason it is non-warrantable is something like too many investor-owned units, and the HOA and building are healthy, it can be a strong buy. The drawbacks are harder financing and less resale liquidity, so the price has to make up for that. It is a case-by-case decision."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can a non-warrantable condo become warrantable again?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Warrantability is a point-in-time status, not a permanent label. A building can become warrantable again once it fixes what caused the problem, such as updating its master insurance policy, resolving litigation, or rebuilding reserves. That is why we check a building's current status rather than relying on what was true a year ago."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I buy a non-warrantable condo as a second home or investment property?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Non-warrantable financing works for primary homes, second homes, and investment properties. The main difference is the loan product and the terms, not whether you are allowed to buy it."}}]}</script>
