 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "@id": "https://chapter3realty.com/invest/strategies/brrrr/#faq", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Does the BRRRR method work in Myrtle Beach?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. It works best on dated single-family homes in inland areas like Conway, Socastee, Carolina Forest, and up toward Longs and Little River. Year-round tenant demand there supports the rent side. Distressed condo units sell at deeper discounts, but HOA rules limit what you can renovate and many oceanfront buildings fail refinance lender tests, so check the building against the refinance before you buy."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the 70 percent rule in BRRRR?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It is a guideline. Keep the purchase price plus rehab budget under about 70 to 75 percent of the fixed-up value. That margin is what lets you refinance later and pull most of your cash back out."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How soon can I refinance a BRRRR property?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Many lenders require seasoning, meaning they make you wait a few months before lending on the new value. Our preferred lender does not require seasoning for BRRRR investors, so there is no waiting period. That makes the after-repair value the deciding number. The refinance comes down to what the house appraises for once the work is done. Know the house, its condition, and what similar homes nearby sold for before you buy. That is what protects your cash."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How much cash can I pull out on the refinance?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Cash-out DSCR refinances are typically capped at 75 to 80 percent of the appraised value. Whether you recover all of your cash depends on how accurate your fixed-up value estimate was."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the biggest risk with BRRRR?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Guessing the fixed-up value too high. If the house appraises low, the refinance returns less cash and more of your money stays stuck. Model the value and rent before you buy."}}]}</script>
