+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/inherited-house/#article","headline":"Selling an Inherited House in South Carolina","description":"Selling an inherited house in South Carolina: probate timing, the stepped-up tax basis, the property tax jump, and whether to sell as-is, repair, or keep it as a rental.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/inherited-house/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-07-19","dateModified":"2026-10-29","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/inherited-house/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"Selling inherited property in South Carolina"},"keywords":"selling inherited house South Carolina, sell inherited property SC, probate house sale Myrtle Beach"}</script>
 <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/inherited-house/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you sell a house while it is still in probate in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually yes. The personal representative can sell estate real estate if the will grants that power or the probate court approves the sale, and sales regularly close while the estate is still open. What you usually cannot do is finish the estate quickly: South Carolina keeps estates open at least eight months so creditors can file claims."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I pay taxes when I sell an inherited house in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"South Carolina has no inheritance tax and no state estate tax. For capital gains, inherited property gets a stepped-up basis: your starting value is the home's fair market value at the date of death, not what the deceased paid. If you sell near that value soon after inheriting, there is often little or no taxable gain. Confirm your numbers with a tax professional."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does probate take in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The minimum is about eight months, because creditors get that long to file claims after notice is published. Straightforward estates commonly wrap up within a year. Disputes are what stretch it: an agent at Chapter3 sold one Myrtle Beach house that a disagreement between heirs had kept in probate for more than six years."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I fix up an inherited house or sell it as-is?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the house, your cash, and your patience. Repairs can add more than they cost on the right house, but they take months and money the estate may not have. Selling as-is is faster and simpler, usually at a lower price. We price both paths on the same house so you can compare real numbers instead of guessing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens to the property taxes on a house I inherit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If the previous owner lived there, the house had South Carolina's 4 percent owner-occupied assessment. It keeps the 4 percent rate until the estate closes, a deed out of the estate is recorded, or December 31 of the year after the death, whichever comes first. It loses it sooner if the house is rented for more than 72 days or its use changes. After that, a house no one lives in is taxed at the 6 percent rate and pays the school operating tax. In Horry County that bill is about 2.5 to 3.8 times the 4 percent bill on the same house. If you move in, apply for the 4 percent rate with the county. Budget for this if you plan to hold the house."}}]}</script>
